  "client_view_text": " 1) Write the database to disk, 2) Write the log to disk.\nThe write-ahead log is a concept in database recovery that involves writing committed transactions to a log file before updating the main database files. Here's how this process helps in case of a crash: When a database modification is made, a record of this modification, along with its commit timestamp, is written to the log file. This log file is kept entirely on disk. The database file itself is updated only after the record of the modification has been written to the log. This ensures that the database file is always consistent and remains in a known state. In other words, if a crash occurs before the update to the database file is complete, the database can be restored to its known state from the log, ensuring that the database remains consistent. Even if the crash occurs after the update to the database file is complete, the log can still be used to recover from any inconsistencies. The write-ahead log effectively provides a record of all committed transactions, which can be used to rebuild the database from the last known good state.\nThe write-ahead log mechanism works as follows: 1) Each transaction is first journalled to the log file, which is kept entirely on disk. This ensures that the log"
